## 1. Saudi-linked assistance and the proposed UMS connection

**Omar al-Bayoumi’s help settling Hazmi and Mihdhar in San Diego is documented.** The evidence for Fahad al-Thumairy’s involvement is more disputed, although a federal judge found it persuasive at the jurisdictional stage in 2025. I found no public, attributable call, payment, correspondence, or job record connecting either man, the other leads below, or the two hijackers to Urban Moving Systems, Max Movers, or their personnel. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}

| Person or lead | What the public record supports |
|---|---|
| **Omar al-Bayoumi** | After the reported February 1, 2000 restaurant encounter, the two men sought him out on February 4. He found an apartment, co-signed the lease, helped them open a bank account and supplied a certified deposit check. The Commission reported immediate reimbursement. He also organized a welcome gathering that Khalid Bin Don videotaped. Bin Don’s April 20, 2004 FBI interview supports the chance-encounter account. Judge Daniels later drew a more adverse inference from the broader evidence. [Commission Report, pp. 216–218](https://www.9-11commission.gov/report/911Report.pdf) · FBI interview, EO14040-000026–000032 · [August 28, 2025 decision, ECF 11182, pp. 37–42](https://wp.nysd.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/2025-08/03-md-01570%2011182.pdf). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="1"} |
| **Fahad al-Thumairy** | The Saudi consular official and King Fahad Mosque imam had documented calls with Bayoumi. Their contents remain unknown. The 2004 Commission found no evidence he assisted the pair. In 2025, Daniels credited Johar’s testimony about an early mosque meeting and Zeid and Alzamari’s testimony about a June 9, 2000 encounter. The judge also cited an FBI-held photograph. I did not independently authenticate that photograph or recover the full depositions. ECF 11182, p. 41, cites plaintiffs’ exhibits 12A, 101, 116 and 117. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="2"} |
| **Musaed al-Jarrah, Adel al-Sadhan and Mutaib al-Sudairy** | Al-Jarrah headed Islamic Affairs at the Saudi embassy. An FBI summary records embassy-number contacts with Thumairy and Bayoumi and raises possible tasking by al-Jarrah. The released material does not supply the conversations or a tasking order. The FBI also considered whether al-Sadhan and al-Sudairy were an advance team. Its overview records no indication of direct contact with the pair, and a February 7, 2023 court order found the showing insufficient to reopen discovery on that theory. FBI EO14040-000227-UPDATED, -000583–000585 and -000591–000596. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"} |
| **Other direct helpers and contacts** | Saudi national **Omar Bakarbashat** acknowledged helping Hazmi with English and taking over the pair’s first apartment. **Yazeed al-Salmi** bought travelers checks in Riyadh, of which Hazmi deposited $1,900 on September 5, 2000 after withdrawing the same amount in cash. The purpose remains unclear. **Mohdar Abdullah** translated, helped with licensing and school applications, and drove the pair to Los Angeles on June 9. The FBI recorded conflicting accounts of his alleged jailhouse admissions. **Abdussattar Shaikh**, an FBI source, rented them a room. **Anwar al-Awlaki** acknowledged meeting Hazmi in San Diego. The Commission suspected he may have directed **Eyad al-Rababah** to help Hazmi and Hani Hanjour in Virginia in 2001. Rababah described a chance meeting. That episode concerns a different pair. [Commission Report, ch. 7](https://911commission.gov/report/911Report_Ch7.htm), especially pp. 218–222 and 229–230 · FBI July 16, 2004 EC, EO14040-000852–000858. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="4"} |
| **Osama Bassnan, Princess Haifa and Prince Bandar** | Payments to Bassnan’s household and the later intelligence-source account about Bandar are separate leads. The Commission’s June 16, 2004 hearing found no evidence that the charitable funds reached the conspiracy. The FBI’s June 14, 2017 *Albayoumi / GIP Cooptee* communication records a source account that Bayoumi received a monthly Saudi intelligence stipend through Bandar. The visible record does not establish the source’s access, document the payments, or show CIA direction. [Senate hearing and FBI exhibit, printed pp. 55–60](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-118shrg53703/pdf/CHRG-118shrg53703.pdf), EO14040-002638–002643. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="5"} |

**The FBI closure and the civil ruling answer different questions.** On May 27, 2021, the FBI and federal prosecutors concluded there was insufficient evidence to prosecute Bayoumi, Thumairy or al-Jarrah for knowingly conspiring to assist the hijackers in furtherance of the attack. On August 28, 2025, Daniels found the evidence sufficient to overcome Saudi Arabia’s immunity challenge, drawing preliminary inferences about coordination, scope of employment and deliberate indifference. The order allowed the civil case to proceed. It did not adjudicate liability. [FBI closure, pp. 3–11, EO14040-000003–000011](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11-attacks-investigation-and-related-materials/9-11-material-released-in-response-to-executive-order-14040/documents-responsive-to-executive-order-14040-section-2-b-ii-part-01-of-01/) · [ECF 11182, pp. 37–45](https://wp.nysd.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/2025-08/03-md-01570%2011182.pdf). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="6"}

**The CIA transmission gap is documented. Its motive remains unresolved.** On January 5, 2000, an FBI detailee drafted a report about Mihdhar’s U.S. visa. A CIA officer recorded a hold. A separate internal cable asserted FBI delivery without identifying the sender, recipient or method. The January 13 follow-up did not produce formal dissemination, and the inspectors general could not verify a separate handoff. Donald Canestraro’s July 20, 2021 declaration, pp. 20–21, lines 723–756, records former agent CS-23’s allegations about CIA records and recruitment through Saudi liaison. It establishes what the source told Canestraro. A contemporaneous tasking instruction, visa-bearing receipt, source-contact report or liaison cable would test the stronger operational claim. [DOJ OIG, pp. 240–250](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/legacy/special/s0606/final.pdf#page=250) · [CIA OIG, pp. 53–55 and 83](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=78) · [Canestraro declaration](https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/23787466-att-b-2021-07-20-donald-canestraro-declaration-dated-20-july-2021/). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="7"}

**The FBI made a real UMS comparison, but the released cover page gives no match.** The September 15, 2001 comparison of addresses and U.S. entry dates for the detainees and hijackers or associates refers to two lists absent from the released cover. The September 14 opening memo’s database hits are redacted. The September 23 Classic International Movers lead names no hijacker customer and supplies no invoice or completed call. An August 10 UMS/Max insurance fax links the two businesses in an insurance record. It supplies no Saudi contact. Newark’s July 10, 2003 assessment found no basis for the advance-knowledge allegations it examined and described a separate headquarters inquiry as still incomplete. The most discriminating records would be the missing comparison lists and underlying subscriber, bank, dispatch or transaction records with both endpoints identified. [Public FBI release scans, archive case 303A-NK-105536](https://archive.org/details/UrbanMovingSystems) · Mirror pagination: §1 p. 1, §2 pp. 13 and 32, §5 pp. 28 and 50, §6 pp. 52–57. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="8"}

## 2. Footage and vehicle location

| Source | What it can establish and where the trail stops |
|---|---|
| **New Jersey Exxon VHS** | The September 28, 2001 Newark referral sent one original surveillance tape and 12 prints for enhancement of two white vans in a displayed **08:29–08:31** interval. The October 18 lab record identifies **K1966 / AGBH009**, a TDK T-160 VHS received October 1, and source prints **K1967–K1978**. Under **011001250 HC JD / JD**, enhancement yielded minimal improvement. Routing accounts for five sets of 13 prints and six CD-R image products. No playable original, recorder-clock test, final plate or vehicle comparison, delivery receipts or item-specific disposition surfaced. [FBI release, §5, archive PDF pp. 69–71 and 74–76](https://archive.org/details/UrbanMovingSystems). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="9"} |
| **Separate Weehawken Exxon lead** | The October 17 New York memo calls the van a possible match and makes its pre-impact inference conditional. It gives no specimen ID, plate, recorder offset or shared frames to tie it to K1966. A September 17 FD-302 also records collection of surveillance for September 10–11 from an unidentified nearby business, with no visible cross-reference to either Exxon record. [Serial 325, printed p. 4](https://www.israellobby.org/UrbanMoving/1169683-001%20---%20303A-NY-C237934-Serial%20325%20---%20Section%201%20%281037758%29.pdf#page=4) · [FBI release, §1 mirror PDF p. 137](https://archive.org/details/UrbanMovingSystems). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="10"} |
| **Tolls, witness and helicopter comparison** | The Newark summary reports a **07:48 Brooklyn–Battery Tunnel E‑ZPass transaction** on the earlier journey. I did not recover the transaction row, lane image or a plate-bearing toll frame. The Doric witness saw smoke before observing the men and recorded **NJ JRJ-13Y** as the van left. The FBI compared a detainee **still photograph** with news-helicopter video and radar, estimating roughly **08:50–09:03**. These are separate anchors. Neither that comparison nor the measured error in the detainees’ Canon still-camera clock calibrates Exxon. Separate Pennsylvania-case receipts put another UMS truck at the Delaware River Toll Bridge at **17:20** and on the Pennsylvania Turnpike at **22:45** on September 10. [Newark FBI release, §1 pp. 63–65, §3 p. 72, §5 pp. 62–63, §6 pp. 47–49, mirror pagination](https://archive.org/details/UrbanMovingSystems) · [Philadelphia FBI, 303C-PH-C91946, Serial 2](https://www.israellobby.org/urbanmoving/1169683-001%20---%20303C-PH-C91946-Serial%202%20---%20Section%201%20%281037749%29.pdf). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="11"} |
| **WTC cameras and Manhattan street/news footage** | A January 1, 2001 installer account describes recorded WTC lobby, plaza and bordering-street CCTV, including rooftop views. This is a specific pre-attack camera lead. It does not identify a retained reel, camera number, recording interval or FBI acquisition. The Smithsonian documents Jules Naudet’s street-level first-impact camera, and the 9/11 Museum documents Gédéon Naudet’s post-impact street filming. NIST’s visual repository provides a further route to event footage. These collections offer discrete fields of view and times. I found no authenticated vehicle match, UMS-specific FBI result from a Manhattan camera, or dated pre-September 11 recording placing either carrier at the complex. [Installer account](https://www.ecmweb.com/content/article/20889492/towering-security) · [Smithsonian, Jules Naudet camera](https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_1003766) · [Museum, Gédéon Naudet camera](https://collection.911memorial.org/Detail/objects/109304/rel/1) · [NIST repository](https://www.nist.gov/world-trade-center-investigation/photos-videos-and-simulations). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="12"} |

A persuasive vehicle match would pair a plate or distinctive features with a verified camera location and calibrated time, then link that vehicle to a carrier and a specific trip. A readable conflicting plate or incompatible body details could exclude a match. For WTC access specifically, a dock entry, receiving record or authenticated gate frame could distinguish a complex visit from lower-Manhattan travel. The clipped visitor-card receipt has no verified issuance or entry transaction. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="13"}

## 3. Dogs, patrols, security and power

**September 6 and September 11 refer to different details in the earliest reporting I located.** The *Los Angeles Times*’ September 12, 2001 wire brief, *Security Alert Was Lifted Only Days Ago*, says bomb-sniffing dogs were removed and quotes Tower One guard Daria Coard saying, “Today [Tuesday] was the first day there was not the extra security.” The brief gives no date for the dogs’ removal. A preserved copy of Curtis L. Taylor and Sean Gardiner’s September 12 *Newsday* report says “on Thursday,” which dates that reported change to **September 6**. Both accounts describe two weeks of 12-hour shifts following phone threats. They share the same named witness. *Loose Change*, second-edition transcript, places the dogs’ removal **and** the end of the extended shifts under September 6, compressing the distinction in the reporting. I located the transcript and a later copy of the *Newsday* text. I did not verify an original print page or a film timecode. [LA Times archive](https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2001-sep-12-mn-44946-story.html) · [Preserved Newsday text](https://groups.google.com/g/alt.sports.football.pro.ne-patriots/c/_MAllntkkf0) · [Second-edition transcript, September 6 entry](https://www.scripts.com/script/loose_change_2nd_edition_12812). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="14"}

**The claim that every explosives-detection dog was removed is contradicted.** Port Authority Police officer David Lim told the Commission on March 31, 2003 that his September 11 assignment was checking trucks for explosives and that he and his explosives-detection dog Sirius were in the B‑1 kennel area of 2 WTC around 08:30. Contemporary April 2002 reporting also documents Sirius’s WTC assignment. That evidence leaves open how many supplemental teams or patrols were withdrawn, which posts changed, and on what dates. Port Authority official Alan Reiss’s May 18, 2004 testimony describes ordinary access controls and the July 2001 management transition, including Silverstein’s own security director. The alert cancellation, threat log, K‑9 rosters, guard overtime sheets and post orders for September 6–11 did not surface. Those records could distinguish Port Authority police coverage from private guard staffing and identify any actual gaps. [Lim statement](https://9-11commission.gov/hearings/hearing1/witness_lim.htm) · [April 25, 2002 LA Times](https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2002-apr-25-mn-39888-story.html) · [Reiss statement, pp. 3–4 and 8–9](https://www.9-11commission.gov/hearings/hearing11/reiss_statement.pdf). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="15"}

**The September 8–9 power-down claim rests on a later tenant account.** The earliest dated version I located is a public reproduction of Scott Forbes’s **April 19, 2004** email. It describes roughly **36 hours above floor 50 in the South Tower**, a tenant IT shutdown and restart, and cabling work as the explanation he was given. The claimed effects on cameras and locks extend beyond the tenant systems he describes working on. A July 20, 2007 posting about his July 18 interview gives **Saturday noon to Sunday 2 p.m.**, or 26 hours, and reports a Port Authority denial. I did not locate the original denial or a switching order. The installer described redundant security networks and emergency generators, which makes the effect of any tenant power outage on particular cameras or locks a separate question. Dated switching orders, tenant notices, contractor sign-ins, and alarm, CCTV or access-control logs would test the outage’s scope and its claimed security effects. [2004 email reproduction](https://www.serendipity.li/wot/forbes01.htm) · [2007 interview notice](https://www.indybay.org/newsitems/2007/07/20/18436524.php) · [January 2001 installer account](https://www.ecmweb.com/content/article/20889492/towering-security). :chatgpt-content-reference{index="16"}

## Search boundaries

- **Saudi links.** I checked the Commission, inspectors general, FBI EO 14040 releases, congressional exhibits, public court materials and contemporary reporting. I cross-checked names and spelling variants against UMS, Max Movers, Suter, Classic and the released FBI comparisons. Redacted database hits and missing attachments limit the negative finding. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="17"}
- **Footage.** I traced the Newark, New York and Philadelphia leads by serial, specimen and lab number and checked NIST and museum catalog leads. Some archive downloads were inaccessible in this pass, so the detailed Newark scan pagination also draws on the earlier public-source review. This was a records and catalog search, without a frame-by-frame audit of every broadcast or recovery of the Exxon media. 
- **Security and power.** I checked contemporary press variants, the second-edition transcript, Commission testimony, NARA’s public finding aid and indexed Port Authority material. I found no date-specific operational records resolving the supplemental alert or alleged shutdown. The New York litigation commonly surfaced in those searches concerns the **1993 bombing** and cannot establish September 2001 staffing. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="19"}

No contacts, records requests, payments or disclosures were made.

